## Executive result

The current V5 inventory already represents **21 of the 25 user assertions** acceptably. Four records require a canonical review patch:

1. Restore **Dimethyl Benzyl Carbinyl Acetate** as the canonical identity for the V5 row currently named “Dimethyl Benzyl Carbonyl Acetate.”
2. Add **Phenyl Ethyl Dimethyl Carbinyl Acetate** as a separate owned, verify-first object.
3. Change **Polysantol** from `HAVE` to `OUT OF STOCK — USER ASSERTED`.
4. Restore the user-specified **Oakmoss Absolute IFRA 10%** product/grade qualifier while leaving safety and exact-grade authority on HOLD until documentation is locked.

**Ambrettolide 10% in DPG remains unchanged:** planned acquisition, design-available for computational architecture, procurement pending for physical compounding.

## Why this is the next high-value nonduplicative task

The broad research and architecture work already exists:

- Clearwood already has a bounded next-action packet and remains direct-gap rank 1.
- The interaction atlas already covers the 23 sensory groups and all group pairs.
- FC2/FC3 and the six-pilot lattice already cover floral and high-complexity architecture.
- The Woody Amber Musk and Top Complexity physical-screening workbooks already contain Polysantol and floral-relay test rows, but those rows remain `NOT RUN`.

The unresolved problem was therefore not another literature map. It was a cross-artifact authority break:

- V5 regressed a previously resolved identity from **Carbinyl** to **Carbonyl**.
- The user’s separate **Phenyl Ethyl Dimethyl Carbinyl Acetate** bottle was absent from V5.
- V5 still marked **Polysantol** as owned despite the user’s explicit out-of-stock statement.
- Downstream priority sheets therefore omitted an 84-formula direct stock gap.

## Identity research

### Dimethyl Benzyl Carbinyl Acetate

Official database and supplier records support:

- canonical name: Dimethyl Benzyl Carbinyl Acetate;
- CAS: `151-05-3`;
- molecular formula: `C12H16O2`;
- PerfumersWorld SKU: `3LF00157`.

Sources:

- https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/9024
- https://www.perfumersworld.com/ifra/view-page.php?pro_id=3LF00157

The physical bottle remains `VERIFY-FIRST`. The user’s raw wording is retained as provenance.

### Phenyl Ethyl Dimethyl Carbinyl Acetate

Official database and supplier records support a distinct object:

- supplier name: Phenyl Ethyl Dimethyl Carbinyl Acetate;
- corresponding structure name: 2-Methyl-4-phenyl-2-butyl acetate;
- CAS: `103-07-1`;
- molecular formula: `C13H18O2`;
- PerfumersWorld SKU: `3LR00160`.

Sources:

- https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/2-Methyl-4-phenyl-2-butyl-acetate
- https://www.perfumersworld.com/ifra/view-page.php?pro_id=3LR00160

This object must not be merged with Dimethyl Benzyl Carbinyl Acetate. Different CAS numbers, molecular formulae, structures and supplier SKUs establish that they are not aliases.

## Polysantol research and direct-gap consequence

The official dsm-firmenich record identifies POLYSANTOL® product `974656`, CAS `107898-54-4`, and describes a deep, diffusive, highly substantive sandalwood profile with natural creamy qualities:

- https://studio.dsm-firmenich.com/product/polysantolr-pe-974656

Primary structure–odor research on sandalwood odorants shows that changes to molecular shape, side-chain substitution and stereochemical configuration can materially change, weaken or erase sandalwood character. This supports preserving Polysantol as an exact target identity rather than assuming Sandalore, Ebanol, Javanol, Sandalwood EO or Sandalwood Base X3 are automatic equivalents.

Relevant primary sources:

- https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules14082780
- https://doi.org/10.1016/S0223-5234(01)01271-5
- https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules17022259
- https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejmech.2006.03.016

These sources do **not** establish that Polysantol will improve liking or similarity in Kenny’s formulas. They support exact-identity preservation and a controlled physical comparison.

